## Promises with dates

You’ve seen how it began. In 1955 the Dartmouth proposal promised a real step forward in one summer. In 1958 Simon and Newell wrote that within ten years a computer would be world chess champion. Money was given on the strength of promises like these, and in the 1960s it was given generously, in the US mostly by the military agency ARPA, later DARPA.

The first warning came from machine translation. In 1966 the American ALPAC committee concluded that machine translation was far from what had been promised, and that a good result shouldn’t be expected soon. The committee spoke of $20 million spent on translation; the historian of machine translation John Hutchins later recalculated it and arrived at nearer $12–13 million. After the report, US funding for translation fell sharply.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what was the ALPAC report about?</summary>

Machine translation: the committee judged it far from what was promised, and after the report US funding for it was cut.

</details>

## The Lighthill report

Britain put the question bluntly. The Science Research Council asked an outsider to assess the state of AI: the mathematician James Lighthill, a Cambridge professor and an expert in fluid mechanics. His report is dated July 1972, and in 1973 the council published it along with replies from several scientists, among them Donald Michie, head of AI research at Edinburgh.

Lighthill divided the field into three parts. A was automation: useful programs for particular tasks. C was computer-based study of the nervous system and psychology. B, the “bridge” between them, was robots and attempts to build a general mind. He thought A and C were sensible work; in B, in his view, the promises had not been kept. His main technical argument he called the combinatorial explosion: the number of options to try snowballs with the size of a problem, so success on a toy problem doesn’t carry over to a real one. You saw that growth in the diagram in the Logic Theorist lesson.

In 1973 Lighthill and his opponents, Michie, the psychologist Richard Gregory and John McCarthy, debated it in a BBC program filmed at the Royal Institution. Sources give the date differently, May or June.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what did Lighthill call the combinatorial explosion?</summary>

The way the number of options snowballs with the size of a problem, so success on a small problem doesn’t carry over to a large one.

</details>

## What happened after the report

You’ll often read that “the Lighthill report killed AI in Britain.” Historians describe it differently. The council reorganized its funding: areas A and C kept getting money, while robotics, area B, was hit hardest, above all Michie’s work at Edinburgh. The historian Jon Agar, in a 2020 paper, shows that the council itself did much to steer the “independent” report, and calls the line about killing AI partly the field’s own legend. Edinburgh’s history of its AI research, written from the inside, speaks of a decade of “massive loss of confidence,” which ended with Britain’s new Alvey program in 1983.

In the US, something similar took its own course. The Mansfield Amendment of 1969 required military money to go to work with a direct and apparent military use. DARPA began to expect reports and results. Its five-year speech understanding program, begun in 1971, ended with no follow-on. The period from about 1974 to 1980 is usually called the first AI winter, though historians draw its edges differently.

Look at the timeline: the events of the two winters and the boom between them.

<WinterTimeline />

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: who was hit hardest after the Lighthill report?</summary>

Robotics, area B, above all Michie’s work at Edinburgh. Other areas kept getting money.

</details>

## The boom and the second winter

In the early 1980s the money came back, and then some. Expert systems began to turn a profit, as XCON did. In 1982 Japan started its Fifth Generation Computer Systems project: machines that were to reason and converse. In response, in 1983 DARPA announced its Strategic Computing program and committed a billion dollars to it over ten years. Special computers were built for expert systems, Lisp machines, made for working in the Lisp language; Symbolics, LMI, Texas Instruments and Xerox made them.

As early as 1984, at the annual conference of the American AI association, AAAI, there was a debate about the “dark ages” of AI. The participants included Roger Schank and John McDermott, the author of XCON, and they warned that the boom would be followed by a bust. The phrase “AI winter” is linked to that debate.

The bust came. Ordinary workstations, Sun’s above all, and then personal computers became cheaper and did as well as Lisp machines. The Lisp machine market collapsed around 1987, and in January 1993 Symbolics filed for bankruptcy protection. Expert systems turned out to be expensive to maintain, as you saw with XCON. DARPA, historians say, cut AI’s share of Strategic Computing from 1987. The Japanese project ended in 1992, and its main goals, machines that reason and converse, were not reached. That’s the second AI winter; historians also differ on when it ended.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why did people stop buying Lisp machines?</summary>

Ordinary workstations and personal computers became cheaper and did as well, and the expert-systems boom was over.

</details>

## What inflated promises end in

The two winters follow one pattern. First comes a promise with a date that’s hard to check. Money is given on the strength of it. The date passes without what was promised, and the money goes all at once, and not only from the empty projects but from the good ones too. Science didn’t stand still: in both winters, work went on that later proved useful. What suffered was trust, and trust takes years to rebuild.

Today AI is rising again, and again there are many promises with dates. This lesson isn’t saying a third winter is coming: there may be none, because today’s models already help millions of people. It’s about how to read promises. The signs of overheating carry on from the questions in the Dartmouth lesson: a date with no way to check it, money for a promise rather than a result, and an expensive solution that others are quickly making cheaper. If you see all three at once, read the forecast calmly and look for what already works today.

## Do it now

1. Find one of today’s forecasts about AI timelines. Note which of the three signs of overheating it shows.
2. Think of an AI tool you use every day, and what would happen to it if the company that makes it ran out of money.
3. Press “Finish the lesson” so the cards go into Review.
